`src/agents.ts:260`. The comment at `src/agents.ts:228-229` and the README both promise that the group \"is also killed if git-gud exits meanwhile\".\n\n**The cause:** the exit hook does nothing: `process.on('exit', () => {})`. Nothing reads `groups` to kill what is left. On Ctrl-C or SIGTERM, `interrupted()` in `src/commands/review.ts:161-164` calls `process.exit(130)`. That runs the empty hook, and every timed reviewer group keeps running.\n\n**Why it is worse than before:** with `--timeout` the agent is spawned with `detached: true`, so it is no longer in the terminal's foreground process group. Ctrl-C therefore no longer reaches the agent at all. The comment at `review.ts:159` (\"the agents die with the terminal's signal\") no longer holds.\n\n**Scenario:** run `git gud review --timeout 30` and press Ctrl-C. git-gud exits and deletes the workspaces. Each agent, plus the commands it started, keeps running in its own group for as long as it likes, possibly in a directory that has been deleted. The `--timeout` limit also stops applying, because its timer died with git-gud.\n\n**What I ran:** `node --test --test-name-pattern=timeout test/gud.test.ts`. The test `--timeout: when git-gud is stopped meanwhile, the reviewer's group dies with it` (`test/gud.test.ts:677`) fails with `AssertionError: the grandchild is gone`. The other three `--timeout` tests pass.\n\n**Fix:** have the hook do `for (const g of groups) killGroup(g)`.\n\n### 2. Minor: a finished reviewer can be reported as timed out and lose its output — **read**\n\n**Where:** `src/agents.ts:261-265, 272`\n\n**The cause:** the timer is cleared only on `close`, and `close` waits until stdout and stderr have been closed by every process holding them.\n\n**Scenario:** the agent writes its review and exits 0, but leaves a background process running (for example a dev server it started for browser checks) that still holds the stdout pipe. `close` never fires. When the limit is reached, the group is killed and the run is reported as `timed out after N min` with `output: ''`. The finished review, already in `stdout` or `outputFile`, is thrown away.\n\nWithout `--timeout` the same situation hangs forever, which was already the case before this change. With `--timeout`, the timeout path should still return the output already collected. A cleaner fix is to end the run on the child's `exit` and then kill the group.\n\n### 3. Minor: on Windows, `--timeout` fails only after the workspaces are built — **read**\n\n**Where:** `src/agents.ts:242`\n\n**The cause:** the `win32` check happens inside `runAgent`, not when the arguments are validated in `review.ts`.\n\n**Scenario:** on Windows, `git gud review --timeout 5` builds every clone, then fails every run one by one with the error message. The command should refuse up front, before any work starts.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
