9640025570","f8f25389fc4877b5","f8f25389fc4877b5","f8f25389fc4877b5","f96057576269ac13","fa9f24fdeb7560b9","fc355a9397884b58","fc89489c6d490b54","fcc38b6fa6777b5f","fe8c92c47899baf3"],"groups":["714933269458edfc","332c9806fa25975d","e6a0f5aeb4bf9877","97dea0a72b2ca198","4bff2ec745429e50","c6e511f3bcf8c53c","54b8db1befc8f144","05d7ac8f869fd608","1c4819f674d980a6","c058bce381d3ee1d","3e5df2accaff17b4","89a762fd208e8717","bdcdaf4d558eb04f","34ffbc485b6b4dfd","c5cc1a46f6a1b688"],"findings":"**Review of the uncommitted `gudfix` snapshot.** I only read the code and grepped it. I did not run anything, so every finding below is **read**. The orchestrator's record (37 pass, mutations red) stands as given.\n\n**1. Mismatch: the failure text can lose its \"timed out\" header (group `timeout`, read)**\n`src/commands/review.ts:205` renders `(res.log || res.output).slice(-4000)`. The timeout log is `timed out after N min; output so far:\\n<partial>`, and the partial output sits at the end. Once the reviewer's partial output passes about 4000 characters, `slice(-4000)` cuts off the header. The README and docs promise the failure text says \"timed out after N min\". The test only uses a short `PARTIAL-WORK`, so it would not catch this. Fix: put the header outside the sliced part, or slice only the partial output.\n\n**2. Mismatch: a TODO item contradicts the code (group `todo`, read)**\nThe TODO says \"a reviewer that finishes normally leaves any background grandchildren that closed their stdio running\". `src/agents.ts` `child.on('exit')` calls `killGroup(pid)` as soon as the leader exits, whatever the stdio state. So those grandchildren are killed, unless they called `setsid`. The test \"left a background process holding the pipe\" asserts the same. The TODO item is wrong and should be removed or reworded.\n\n**3. Missing risk: SIGHUP is not covered by the exit hook (group `timeout`, read)**\nThe exit hook only runs when Node runs its `exit` event. `review.ts:167-168` handles only SIGINT and SIGTERM. Closing the terminal sends SIGHUP, and Node's default for that signal ends the process without emitting `exit`. A timed reviewer is detached, so it has no terminal hangup to receive. It keeps running until its own timeout, which is up to a week. The docs cover SIGKILL but not SIGHUP. Fix: add `process.once('SIGHUP', interrupted)`, and list it in the docs and TODO.\n\n**4. Missing risk: a timed reviewer has no controlling terminal (group `timeout`, read)**\n`detached: timed` in `src/agents.ts` makes the reviewer a session leader (`setsid`), not only a process-group leader. A timed reviewer that needs `/dev/tty`, such as a git credential or ssh passphrase prompt, will fail, while the same reviewer without `--timeout` works. The docs say only \"own process group\", so this difference is undocumented. Worth a sentence in the README.\n\n**5. Minor, read: the partial output is not cleaned before being shown**\nOn timeout, `written()` reads the output file or stdout. That text reaches the failure block without `cleanText`. The same is true of the existing stderr path. The section says reviewer text is treated as data, so this is an inconsistency, not a new hole.\n\n**Checked and consistent (read)**\n- `clean-text` regex: alternation order and the `(?!\\x1b)` lookahead give the claimed results for `a\\x1b\\x1b[31mred` → `ared` and `a\\x1b\\x1bred` → `aed`. The OSC case also checks out.\n- `evidence-stage`: `cleanLabel` is applied to stage names before they are echoed. Windows is refused during argument parsing, before any workspace is created.\n- Signal path: SIGINT and SIGTERM call `process.exit(130)`, which runs the exit hook, so the SIGINT/SIGTERM tests match the code.\n- `improve-wording`: the prompt change matches the rules block.\n- `honesty-sep-test`: the fixture matches the `honesty` stage's setup.\n\nThe author's risk list covers SIGKILL of git-gud and the pid-reuse race. Those match the code and are fine.\n\n**Necessity:** not graded, per the brief.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
