025570","f8f25389fc4877b5","f8f25389fc4877b5","f8f25389fc4877b5","f96057576269ac13","fa9f24fdeb7560b9","fc355a9397884b58","fc89489c6d490b54","fcc38b6fa6777b5f","fe8c92c47899baf3"],"groups":["714933269458edfc","332c9806fa25975d","e6a0f5aeb4bf9877","97dea0a72b2ca198","4bff2ec745429e50","c6e511f3bcf8c53c","54b8db1befc8f144","05d7ac8f869fd608","1c4819f674d980a6","c058bce381d3ee1d","3e5df2accaff17b4","89a762fd208e8717","bdcdaf4d558eb04f","34ffbc485b6b4dfd","c5cc1a46f6a1b688"],"findings":"I found one defect that needs fixing. The rest of the change held up on reading and on the one run I did.\n\n### 1. `--timeout` leaves reviewers running when git-gud is hung up (SIGHUP) — major, **verified**\n\n**Where:** `src/agents.ts:249` (`detached: timed`), together with the exit hook at `src/agents.ts:263`. The only signal handlers are SIGINT and SIGTERM, at `src/commands/review.ts:167-168`.\n\n**What goes wrong:** With `--timeout`, each reviewer runs in its own process group. The cleanup that kills those groups is a `process.on('exit')` hook, and it only runs on a normal exit or through the SIGINT/SIGTERM handler, which calls `process.exit`. Node's default action for SIGHUP ends the process without running `exit` handlers. Two things then go wrong:\n- The shell sends SIGHUP to git-gud's process group when the terminal closes or an SSH session drops. The reviewer is no longer in that group, so it never gets the signal.\n- git-gud dies, and the deadline timer dies with it. Nothing enforces the time limit any more.\n\nThe reviewer is left orphaned with no limit, running with the user's agent permissions (and API spend) until it decides to stop. Without `--timeout`, the same hangup kills it. So this is a regression for exactly the users who asked for a limit, and it isn't the case the TODO lists (\"a SIGKILL of git-gud itself\").\n\n**What I ran:** A stand-in `claude` that runs `exec sleep 300`, with git-gud started under `setsid`, then `kill -HUP -- -<git-gud pgid>`:\n- **Without `--timeout`:** git-gud and the reviewer are in one group (`2854578`), and after the HUP the group is gone.\n- **With `--timeout=5`:** git-gud dies of the hangup, but the reviewer (`2853618`, its own group, `SNs`) is still running a second later.\n\n**Fix:** Handle SIGHUP the way SIGINT and SIGTERM are handled (and ideally SIGQUIT too). The README sentence that says the exit handler covers \"Ctrl-C or SIGTERM\" should also say what happens on a hangup.\n\n### Checked and found sound (from reading only)\n- **Run outcome:** The `exit` → 2s grace → `close` sequence resolves only once. A run that finishes just before the deadline clears the timer, and a timed-out run reports `ok: false` with an empty output.\n- **Sandboxed runs:** These use `--die-with-parent --unshare-pid`, so killing the bwrap process group also tears down everything inside the sandbox.\n- **Time limit:** The largest accepted value, 10080 min, is 604,800,000 ms, well within the timer's range.\n- **`cleanText`:** Rewriting it as a single pass doesn't remove anything the old passes kept. A lone ESC is still removed with the control characters.\n- **`--evidence-stage`:** It is applied per prompt correctly. A stage that is named but doesn't run just gets nothing.\n\nI made no changes to the code.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
