a8e3339b24","d13728ad049a4d70","d1ad85efd9255f63","d3d77fd97139560d","d4f41d63b23b2587","d6e5498cf1dac0ad","d6fe45a4fc522bbe","d8c987dc31d5c3ee","db0c03bf0dc4bd00","dc10e30de8cfa72e","dc42a79d2437918b","e209fc7b37ce752b","e3835d40629fd425","e4476ab93cd90d5d","e57abcd67460006a","e5db01dbf9be3bc7","e73372c2c569be40","e8326de1337ead5c","e9e7d578f4efef96","e9e7d578f4efef96","e9e7d578f4efef96","e9e7d578f4efef96","ea564bc17b017562","eb206458f52dc6f1","eb448d9c5cfed9aa","eb448d9c5cfed9aa","ec639f31f71a7c8d","eea26e1f9e894aec","eed785f56dbb3957","ef3c3299d76fac07","f1632d96be32762a","f25ad7b34c493123","f2fc88986dfa6250","f39931b0c6f76574","f4012290a65b653e","f440166ece372582","f468be1199c2dd8a","f4aa9deda545a357","f5574e75fd131991","f56f430657cc89ae","f60efdb63c2ca21c","f6ed0e387f176728","f807d63568d9b8f9","f8441754f5f74eaf","f886fe9640025570","f8f25389fc4877b5","f8f25389fc4877b5","f8f25389fc4877b5","f96057576269ac13","fa9f24fdeb7560b9","fc355a9397884b58","fc89489c6d490b54","fcc38b6fa6777b5f","fe8c92c47899baf3"],"groups":["714933269458edfc","332c9806fa25975d","e6a0f5aeb4bf9877","97dea0a72b2ca198","4bff2ec745429e50","c6e511f3bcf8c53c","54b8db1befc8f144","d0a15591c3bac880","1c4819f674d980a6","c058bce381d3ee1d","3e5df2accaff17b4","89a762fd208e8717","bdcdaf4d558eb04f","34ffbc485b6b4dfd","d545bc3daf8cdcf5","6b4fa156d854d7c3","c5cc1a46f6a1b688"],"findings":"**Mismatches**\n\n1. **`timeout` notes vs code: pid signalling after reap (read).** The note says \"Group ids are removed on close and on timeout so a pid is not signalled after it was reaped.\" The `exit` handler in `src/agents.ts` (inside `if (limit !== undefined && pid)`) calls `killGroup(pid)` and `groups.delete(pid)`. The `exit` event fires after the leader is reaped, so the code does signal by pid after reaping. The TODO admits this (\"killGroup(pid) after the leader is gone\"), but the group note says the opposite. The note should say the exit path signals after reap, and that it is safe only while the group has members or the pid is unused.\n\n2. **README example silently changed (read).** `README.md:50` used to be `git gud review wip --stage honesty --agent claude:haiku --evidence …`. The new example drops `--stage honesty`, so it now runs `bugs`, `honesty` and `improve`: three agent runs instead of one. That is more cost than the example's purpose, which was a single honesty review. The `docs` group says only that the README documents the new flags, so this change is not in its stated intent. Either restore `--stage honesty` and add `--evidence-stage honesty`, or say the example now runs all stages.\n\n3. **Partial output can start with a lone surrogate (verified).** `src/agents.ts` does `cleanText(written()).slice(-3000)`. The slice counts UTF-16 code units, so a cut inside an astral character leaves a lone low surrogate at the start. I ran `('\\u{1F600}' + 'a'.repeat(2999)).slice(-3000)`: the first code unit is `de00`, a lone low surrogate. The `timeout` group does not list this. It is cosmetic, but it contradicts \"cut to its last 3000 characters\" if read as whole characters. Fix: cut on code points (e.g. `Array.from(s).slice(-3000).join('')`).\n\n**Checked and matching**\n\n- **`clean-text` (verified).** I ran the single-pass regex from the diff in Node on all seven cases in `clean-text-test`: `ared`, `aed`, `a`, `a\\nb`, `xy`, `ab`, and empty. All match. The new alternation does not join an earlier ESC to a later character, and the lone ESC is removed by the control-character class. Pre-change, `a\\x1b\\x1b[31mred` gave `aed`; this is the intended fix.\n- **`evidence-stage` (read).** The `cleanLabel` path for `--evidence-stage` and `--stage` errors matches the test expectations. The `evidenceStages` filter is applied only in `stagePrompt`, and empty or unknown values error out. The note goes to stderr only when `--evidence` is absent.\n- **`improve-wording` (read).** The prompt line now says \"ran the relevant single test file(s)\", which agrees with the rules block.\n- **`timeout` parse and signals (read).** The number regex rejects the listed hostile inputs. The SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGHUP/SIGQUIT list gives exit codes 130/130/129/131, and `process.exit` runs the exit hook that kills the groups. The failure block takes `(res.log || res.output).slice(-4000)`, so the 3000-character tail plus the header fits, as the notes say.\n- **`honesty-sep-test` and `evidence-stage-tests` (read; not re-run).** These match the code paths. I did not re-run them, since the orchestrator's `npm test` evidence covers them.\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n- **Timed reviewer and Ctrl-C (read).** A timed reviewer is detached, so the terminal's Ctrl-C no longer reaches it. Ctrl-C now depends entirely on the `exit` hook, which runs only if git-gud reaches `process.exit`. The author's SIGKILL risk covers this, but the README should also say that a timed run with a stuck `ws.dispose()` leaves the reviewer running until git-gud is killed.\n- **Escaped reviewers after a normal finish (read).** After the leader exits, git-gud waits 2 s and then releases the pipes. Output still streaming from an escaped process is cut, and the run is reported `ok`. This is listed as a risk, but it means a truncated review can be presented as complete.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
