eted but never read on exit. The code comment at `agents.ts:228-229`, the README paragraph (\"The group is also killed if git-gud exits meanwhile\"), and the `timeout` notes all claim behaviour that does not exist. I ran the test `--timeout: when git-gud is stopped meanwhile, the reviewer's group dies with it` in isolation (`node --test --test-name-pattern \"stopped meanwhile\" test/gud.test.ts`). It fails at `assert.ok(await gone(child), 'the grandchild is gone')` (test/gud.test.ts:690). The grandchild `sleep 300` survives after git-gud is SIGTERMed. This contradicts the implementer's evidence that the test passes and that removing the exit hook turns a test red. The test result is authoritative on this machine; the implementer may have run with a different environment. Either way, the code path is a no-op regardless of environment (**read** for the no-op, **verified** for the failing test).\n\n2. **`timeout`: the failure mode the exit hook was meant to prevent is live.** Because the reviewer runs with `detached: true`, it no longer receives the terminal's Ctrl-C (the code comment admits this). The timer lives in git-gud's process. If git-gud is killed, the reviewer keeps running with no limit. Item 1 means nothing cleans it up. This is the main missing risk (**verified** through the failing test above).\n\n3. **`timeout`: a risk the author did not list.** Detaching removes terminal Ctrl-C from the reviewer. The author's risk list covers setsid escape, SIGKILL without flush, and background grandchildren. It omits this one. The TODO and README also do not mention it (**read**).\n\n4. **`timeout`: dead state.** The `groups` Set is populated and pruned but never consulted for anything except its own bookkeeping. Once item 1 is fixed (exit handler kills every tracked group), the set becomes useful. Until then it is dead weight (**read**).\n\n5. **`evidence-stage` / docs: the \"wip\" example is not wrong, but check the claim that `--evidence` reaches `bugs` by default.** This is consistent with the code (`evidenceStages = stages` by default). No mismatch here (**read**).\n\n**Not checked, due to budget:** the other new tests (`--timeout kills the reviewer's whole process group`, `runAgent: no timeout...`, `--evidence-stage` tests, `honesty-sep-test`). I did not run them. The `cleanText` regex I traced by hand against the three ESC cases in `clean-text-test` and they match the expected outputs (**read**). The `improve-wording` change is a one-line prompt edit, consistent with its claim (**read**).\n\n**Simplifications**\n\n- Once the exit hook is real, `groups` can be a plain `Set` read in one `process.on('exit')` handler (`killGroup` for each pid). Note that `exit` handlers are synchronous, which suits `process.kill`. For Ctrl-C/SIGTERM you need `process.on('SIGINT'/'SIGTERM')` handlers that kill the groups and then re-raise, because `exit` does not fire on signals by default (**read**). This is the missing piece the author tried to implement.\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n- Item 2 and item 3 above.\n- A timed-out run returns `ok: false` with `output: ''`, so the reviewer's partial output is discarded even when it was written to `outputFile`. The author's SIGKILL risk mentions flush, but the code also never reads the output file on timeout (**read**).\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
